# ic.slex -- the Iced Coffee token language.  THIS is your lexer:
# this file plus the TokenKind enum (src/main/scala/ic/lex/Token.scala)
# describes every token of IC.
#
#   PATTERN { scala-code }   run the code on the matched lexeme;
#                            `text` and `pos` are in scope; produce
#                            the Token or call error(...)
#   PATTERN                  no action: match and discard
#
# Two tie-breakers, exactly as in lex (and HW 2): each token is the
# LONGEST match possible at the current position (maximal munch), and
# if several rules match that same longest lexeme, the EARLIEST rule
# wins.  Because of longest match, most rules can go in any order --
# `<=` beats `<` no matter where each rule sits -- and order only
# decides between rules that match the SAME lexeme.

%kinds ic.lex.TokenKind

# Fixed-text tokens: each row declares its kind AND places a rule for
# its literal text at this spot in the rule order.
#
# TODO: add every keyword, operator, and punctuation mark of IC
# (follow the lexical rules in the IC specification).  A keyword row
# anywhere above the identifier rule beats it on ties -- first rule
# wins -- so no keyword table is needed anywhere.
%token
  LPAREN "("   RPAREN ")"

# whitespace produces no tokens (no action = match and discard)
# TODO: also skip `//` line comments here.
[ \t\r\n]+

# C-style (non-nested) block comments, provided: after `/*`, any run
# of non-`*` characters or of stars not followed by `/`, then one or
# more stars and the closing `/`.
/\*([^*]|\*+[^*/])*\*+/

# TODO: identifiers and class identifiers: one rule whose action
# picks ID or CLS_ID from the lexeme.  (Add the kinds to the
# TokenKind enum first: every name this spec uses must be a member
# of the enum, and the enum's members are automatically usable in
# the blocks below.)

# TODO: integer literals: the regex finds the digits; the action
# enforces the non-regular parts (no leading zeros, 32-bit range),
# calling error(...) when they fail.

# TODO: string literals: the regex matches the entire literal --
# quotes and the legal escapes \t \n \" \\ -- and the action converts
# the escapes, so a STRINGLIT token's text is the string's *contents*.

# TODO: error rules.  A rule that matches only when nothing better
# does turns "no rule matches" into a precise message: e.g. a lone
# `"` (a complete string literal is a longer match, so this rule only
# wins when the literal never closes) should report the beginning of
# an unterminated string literal, and a final catch-all should report
# any invalid input character.

# an example error action; replace it when you make + a real token
\+                       { error("TODO: '+' is not a token yet") }
